WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.020
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:01.780
So when I was a kid,

00:00:01.780 --> 00:00:04.700
there was a popular joke construction called the Russian reversal.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.420
The first time I heard a Russian reversal

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.900
was in a Miller Lite commercial in 1985

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.980
where the comedian Yakov Smirnoff said

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:13.460
In America, you can always find a party.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:16.900
In Soviet Russia, party always finds you.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.700
But the joke is actually much older than that.

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:21.740
It was used in a 1938 Cole Porter musical,

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.640
and in a 1960s episode of the show Laugh-In where the joke was:

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.320
In America everybody watches television.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.920
In the old country, television watches you.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:32.060
Ha! That's funny!

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.400
Everyone said before our televisions started gathering

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.460
reams of information about our viewing habits.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.220
Be careful who you laugh at, friends,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.079
for in time, you may become them.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:40.660
But, right,

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.700
Russian reversal jokes were basically low-level,

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.740
xenophobic weapons of the Cold War.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.320
Trying to get Americans to believe

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.700
that their country where you could always find a party

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.460
and watch television,

00:00:49.460 --> 00:00:51.700
was perfect, in purported contrast

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:53.300
to life in the Soviet Union.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.460
There were, of course, also American reversal jokes.

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:56.260
My favorite being:

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:58.900
In the Soviet Union, people sometimes rob banks.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.200
In capitalist America, bank robs you.

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:03.640
And there were jokes that refuted both ideologies,

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.680
like this one from Moldovan-American educator Emil Robbie:

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.820
Under capitalism man exploits man.

00:01:09.980 --> 00:01:12.780
Under communism, it's the other way around.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.300
Of course, these days, the Russian reversal has been memed to near-extinction,

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.660
like so much else on the internet.

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.360
But, I still find the joke construction really, really interesting

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.080
because, it starts out with you having agency

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:23.960
over something

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.460
and then ends up with that something having agency over you.

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:29.000
Like in a way Michael Pollan's famous

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.460
observation about corn is a Russian reversal joke.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.860
He pointed out that, while it's true that corn has adapted to our human interests,

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:38.000
if you look at it from corn's perspective,

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:41.560
we have adapted a lot to corns' interests.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:44.660
Like, we've changed our habitats and our diets and our travel patterns

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.000
to make corn more plentiful

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.320
and when you look at it that way,

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.040
corn kind of becomes the farmer

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.780
and we kind of become the crop.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:52.220
And when you start looking for these jokes,

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.540
you see them everywhere.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.060
Like, for starters, in Shakespeare.

00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:56.660
In Richard II, the king says:

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:59.560
I wasted time, and now time doth waste me.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:00.949
There's a Winnie the Pooh story

00:02:00.949 --> 00:02:03.180
where Piglet says he's so small that he doesn't fly a kite,

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:04.980
so much as the kite flies him.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:07.280
And in the Oscar-nominated feature film Boss Baby,
[Oh John....]

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.860
the titular character says, "Either you run the day,

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:11.220
or the day runs you."

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.780
Slightly off-topic, but the book that movie is adapted from is excellent.

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:18.400
Anyway, I used this construction a lot in my book Turtles All the Way Down:

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:20.980
You're the storyteller, but you're also the story told,

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:21.980
you think you're spending money,

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.000
but the money is spending you, et cetera

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.260
Because the book is about what happens when

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.240
you feel like you're not captaining the ship of your so-called self.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.420
And I think what I love most about Russian reversals is

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.840
that they can reveal how weak we are

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:37.180
before the forces of time and the wind and money and everything else.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.800
And in general I find it helpful to take

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:40.760
a kind of Russian reversal style look at

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.180
any big force in our lives.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.100
Like, take the social internet's algorithms, for instance.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:48.000
Are they informing us, or are we informing them?

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:48.880
Or free markets:

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.920
Do markets exist to make human lives better and more efficient,

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.860
or do human lives exist to make markets better and more efficient?

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.240
Of course, the Russian reversal can create a false dichotomy.

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:00.700
You can waste time 𝑎𝑛𝑑 be wasted by time.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:01.880
And, as we all know now,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.260
you can watch television

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:05.300
and be watched by your television.

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.240
And maybe there's no tension at all in some reversals.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:10.160
Like, I guess it's possible that whatever makes markets more efficient

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:11.460
is good for human lives

00:03:11.460 --> 00:03:14.520
and whatever is good for human lives makes markets more efficient.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.640
But in my experience, anyway,

00:03:15.640 --> 00:03:18.480
that kind of ideological rigidity rarely holds up

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.660
to careful scrutiny.

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.220
And I like the Russian reversal

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:24.080
because it asks us to consider where the power

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:26.300
is really centered in our discourse.

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.660
Sometimes the answer is obvious,

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:28.980
the humans who plant the corn

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.250
have much more power than the corn that gets planted.

00:03:31.250 --> 00:03:32.360
But sometimes it isn't;

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.280
is the real power with the Twitter user

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:35.600
who chooses what they tweet

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:36.680
and who they follow

00:03:36.680 --> 00:03:40.579
or with the algorithm that decides which tweets the user actually sees?

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.520
And is the real power with the people who make markets work

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:46.009
or with the markets that make people work?

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:47.660
I don't think those questions have easy answers,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.700
but I do think that looking at them backwards has a way of telling us

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.029
truths we otherwise might miss.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.020
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

